      "worked": "<strong>A three-minute outline for: 'Impact of Social Media on Students'</strong><br><strong>Introduction:</strong> Hook — an average student checks the phone over a hundred times a day. Background — social media is now part of daily student life. Main idea: social media affects students both positively and negatively, and the outcome depends on how it is used.<br><strong>Body 1 — Benefit:</strong> access to learning material, doubt-solving groups, career information.<br><strong>Body 2 — Benefit:</strong> connection and communication skills, exposure to opportunities.<br><strong>Body 3 — Harm:</strong> loss of study time and broken concentration.<br><strong>Body 4 — Harm:</strong> comparison with others causing anxiety and low confidence.<br><strong>Conclusion:</strong> restate that the tool is neutral; suggest fixed usage hours and phone-free study blocks.<br><br><strong>Sample introduction written from that outline:</strong><br>'An average student today checks his phone more than a hundred times a day. Social media has become part of student life as naturally as textbooks once were. It brings genuine benefits, such as easy access to study material, but also real harm, such as broken concentration. Its final effect depends less on the platform than on how carefully the student uses it.'",
